    of the value in the frame of the function that converts it (here Fine, at
    main.p:134), and the interface points at that copy. fmt.Println passes a
    pointer reachable from args to Fprintln (print_linux.p:17), which passes a
    pointer reachable from args to (*printer).doPrint (fmt.p:948), which passes
    a pointer reachable from args to (*printer).printArg (fmt.p:915), which
    passes arg to a formatting method of what it walks (which may call
    main.(W).String) (fmt.p:671), which stores w in package variable keep
    (main.p:28), so the interface would outlive Fine's frame.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change fmt.Println to take a value or an owner, which it can keep: an
    owned[T] from new, a shared[T], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
